name: exit status and the special variables
boss: AndOr
flavor: Exit zero and you pass. Anything else is mine.
syntax: ||, $?, $$, $!, &
brief: Every command returns a number. Zero means success; anything else is a
brief: failure the command chose. $? holds the last one.
brief:
brief: That number is what && and || branch on. cmd1 && cmd2 runs the second
brief: only if the first succeeded; cmd1 || cmd2 only if it failed. This is
brief: the smallest conditional the shell has, and the most used.
brief:
brief: The other specials: $$ this shell's pid, $! the last background pid,
brief: $# how many arguments, $@ all of them, $0 the name it was invoked as.
---
q: {run false, then %show% the exit status it left behind in $?|run false and check what $? comes back as|execute false, then %show% its exit code from $?}
a: false; echo $?
cmp: ref
ref: false
teach: false
hint: false always exits 1; it has no other job
---
q: {run true, then %show% the exit status it left behind in $?|run true and verify $? comes back as 0|execute true, then %show% its exit code from $?}
a: true; echo $?
cmp: ref
ref: true
teach: true
hint: true always exits 0, no matter what
---
q: %show% @word@ only if @log@ contains @sev@
a: grep -q @sev@ @log@ && echo @word@
out: @word@
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: -q silences grep's output, leaving only its exit status for &&
---
q: %show% "missing" if @log@ has no match for nonexistent
a: grep -q nonexistent @log@ || echo missing
out: ^missing$
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: || runs its right side only when the left side fails
---
q: %show% found if @needle@ is in @log@, else missing
a: grep -q @needle@ @log@ && echo found || echo missing
cmp: ref
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: chain && and || together to cover both outcomes in one line
---
setup: a pid-named lockfile keeps two copies of a script from colliding
q: %show% this shell's pid to use in the lockfile name
a: echo $$
out: ^[1-9][0-9]*$
ref: sh
teach: sh
hint: $$ is this shell's own pid, fixed for its whole lifetime
---
q: {start sleep 1 in the background and print the pid it was given|background sleep 1, then print its pid|run sleep 1 as a background job and show what pid it got}
a: sleep 1 & echo $!
out: ^[1-9][0-9]*$
ref: sleep
teach: sleep
hint: an ampersand backgrounds; $! holds the pid it was just given
---
q: {exit 0 so a caller checking $? sees success|end the shell with a clean, successful exit status|leave the shell reporting success via exit status 0}
a: exit 0
status: 0
ref: exit
teach: exit
hint: exit takes the status as its argument; with none it reuses $?
---
q: {exit with status 3 so a caller checking $? knows the failure|end the shell with exit status 3, a custom failure code|leave the shell with exit status 3, a signal to the caller}
a: exit 3
status: 3
ref: exit
teach: exit
hint: any number 0-255; zero means success and everything else is yours to define
---
q: {succeed only when @log@ has no match for nonexistent|flip a status so no match for nonexistent in @log@ succeeds|make a missing nonexistent match in @log@ count as success}
a: ! grep -q nonexistent @log@
status: 0
ref: grep
teach: grep -q
hint: a leading ! flips the status
---
q: %make% a broken step stop the whole script instead of continuing silently
a: set -e
why: conceptual
ref: set
teach: set -e
hint: -e makes any failing command end the script immediately
---
q: %make% a typo'd variable name fail loudly instead of expanding to empty
a: set -u
why: conceptual
ref: set
teach: set -u
hint: -u turns a reference to an unset variable into a hard error
---
q: {cd into @word@-place, or create it if that fails|enter @word@-place, falling back to creating it if cd fails|cd into @word@-place or, failing that, make it}
a: cd @word@-place || mkdir @word@-place
state: exists:@word@-place
ref: cd
teach: cd
hint: || only runs its right side when the left side's exit status is nonzero -- cd fails cleanly when the target is missing
---
q: %make% the shell show each command as it runs, for debugging
a: set -x
why: conceptual
ref: set
teach: set -x
hint: this is the shell debugger, and it is usually enough
---
q: %turn% command tracing back off
a: set +x
why: conceptual
ref: set
teach: set
hint: + turns an option off, the mirror of - turning it on
---
q: %show% the shell's current option settings
a: set -o
out: errexit
ref: set
teach: set -o
hint: -o with no name lists every option and whether it's on or off
